---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('privy')!;
const b = appBySlug('drip')!;
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-drip';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/privy-vs-drip');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/privy-vs-drip');
const faqs = [
  { question: 'Privy or Drip for cart recovery?', answer: 'Evaluate Privy when signup offers and the messages following them need one operating home. Evaluate Drip when purchase-history segments and their recovery branches are the main design requirement. Both need a store-specific demonstration; these are evaluation priorities, not exclusive capabilities.' },
  { question: 'Should Privy capture subscribers while Drip sends?', answer: 'Only retain that split if the connection preserves identity, the offer shown, permission and suppression state. Assign one welcome sender and one recovery owner, and test what happens when the same shopper changes their address or unsubscribes.' },
  { question: 'Does connecting Drip activate recovery?', answer: 'Drip says the connection itself does not start automated emails. Complete the sync, review the available data and configure and test the intended workflows separately.' },
  { question: 'How should an incentive be evaluated?', answer: 'For a hypothetical $399 basket with 46% product gross margin, product cost is $215.46. An 18% discount reduces revenue to $327.18 and product gross profit to $111.72, before shipping, fees, returns and messaging costs.' },
  { question: 'What can change migration costs?', answer: 'Drip documents that existing Shopify customers with email addresses sync into Drip and that the added customers affect billing. Reconcile the resulting audience and current commercial terms before comparing costs with the Privy configuration you would replace.' },
  { question: 'How do we judge a recovery trial?', answer: 'Check offer consistency, correct segment entry, current cart content and exit after purchase or suppression. Separately measure incremental product gross profit using an appropriate randomized comparison; attributed orders alone do not establish lift.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/compare" class="link text-sm font-bold">← Comparisons</a>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Choose around the work your team needs to improve: connecting signup offers to recovery, or building recovery branches from customer purchase history.</p>
      <div class="mt-10">
        <TldrBox picks={[{ label: 'Capture and follow-up', tool: a.name, reason: 'Evaluate the signup offer and the journey it starts together.' }, { label: 'Purchase-history branches', tool: b.name, reason: 'Evaluate the Shopify data behind each intended recovery segment.' }]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Compare overlapping tools through a real decision</h2>
        <p>Privy presents popups, email, SMS and visual customer journeys. Drip documents Shopify customer and order data for segmentation and automation. Calling this basic capture versus advanced automation hides the overlap. Start with the workflow that currently consumes operating time: correcting an offer mismatch after signup, or maintaining different messages for first-time and repeat purchasers.</p>
        <p>A store running one welcome offer and a straightforward cart reminder should evaluate whether Privy can keep that promise coherent from form to message. A store with distinct purchase-history journeys should evaluate the fields and branches in Drip before moving them. The second requirement does not make the first store unsophisticated, and neither requirement proves a revenue winner.</p>
        <p>Sources checked November 24, 2026: <a href="https://www.privy.com/">Privy's product overview</a> and <a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip's Shopify connection guide</a>. These establish published scope, not results from this site's testing. Plan availability and actual installation behavior still require account-level verification.</p>
        <h2>Two useful demonstrations</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Privy demonstration</th><th>Drip demonstration</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Offer continuity</td><td>Show the signup promise and the subsequent welcome and recovery messages.</td><td>Show where the captured offer becomes usable data for the recovery branch.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Repeat customer treatment</td><td>Build the intended exception for an existing customer receiving a new signup offer.</td><td>Build the intended purchase-history segment and inspect who qualifies.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Operating work</td><td>Edit the offer once and identify every form and message requiring a matching change.</td><td>Change a segment condition and identify which journeys and queued contacts are affected.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h2>Proposed trial: the returning buyer who signs up again</h2>
        <p>Use test recipients representing a new shopper and a returning purchaser. Give both the same basket, then submit the planned signup form. These are proposed checks, not observed merchant outcomes. Record the displayed offer, contact identity, permission source, purchase history, selected branch and queued messages.</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>New shopper:</strong> confirm that the promised benefit reaches the intended address. Inspect whether recovery uses that offer or introduces a conflicting incentive. A signup should not create two competing welcome sequences.</li>
          <li><strong>Returning purchaser:</strong> confirm whether the historical order is available before the segment decision. If history arrives later, inspect whether the shopper remains in the wrong branch. Decide whether to delay entry until the needed data is available or use a safe fallback message.</li>
          <li><strong>Basket update:</strong> change the variant and quantity while a message waits. Inspect the resulting content and destination rather than assuming a template refreshes automatically. Do not send a reminder for an obsolete basket.</li>
          <li><strong>Purchase or unsubscribe:</strong> make each change while recovery is pending. Trace how the active branch and any separate sender receive the change. A segment match at entry does not prove eligibility at dispatch.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Drip distinguishes the store connection from activating automated emails. Its guide also separates the data sync from the app embed used for page and product-view tracking, with a qualification for stores where that embed option is absent. Diagnose missing history and missing browsing activity separately. Neither a successful connection banner nor a populated contact list proves that all the intended triggers work.</p>
        <h2>Keep a split capture setup only when its benefit survives the handoff</h2>
        <p>If Privy forms already convert useful traffic and Drip owns established journeys, replacing both at once may add unnecessary work. First map how a signup reaches Drip, including the offer version and permission record. This is a proposed architecture to validate, not a claim of an automatic Privy-to-Drip connection. Assign ownership for retries and failures; do not enable a second welcome sender just because it is available.</p>
        <p>For a cutover, inventory active forms, signup benefits, recovery entries and pending messages. Reconcile existing subscribers and suppression records before enabling the replacement sender. Choose a transition window based on actual queue duration and synchronization behavior rather than an arbitrary number of weeks. Retain a rollback plan that prevents old pending reminders from resuming unexpectedly.</p>
        <h2>Discounts reduce revenue, not product cost</h2>
        <p>Hypothetical example: a $399 basket at 46% product gross margin has $215.46 product cost and $183.54 full-price product gross profit. An 18% discount costs $71.82 in revenue. With unchanged goods cost, discounted revenue is $327.18 and product gross profit is $111.72. It is not $150.50: that would incorrectly discount the original gross profit instead of revenue.</p>
        <p>This is before shipping subsidies, payment fees, returns and messaging costs. Use the same offer economics in both trials. A repeat buyer who would have returned anyway can receive an unnecessary subsidy even when a dashboard attributes the order to recovery. Randomize an eligible comparison group where feasible, choose sample size and duration from baseline conversion and the effect you need to detect, and compare profit after discounts and costs. No fixed holdout percentage establishes a valid result for every store.</p>
        <h2>Quote the audience you will actually import</h2>
        <p>Request current quotes for the same audience, sending volume and required functionality. Include capture tools retained alongside the sender, SMS charges if used, integration work and operator time. Avoid comparing a starting price for one product with a fully configured program for the other.</p>
        <p>Drip's Shopify guide warns that importing existing customers with email addresses affects billing. Reconcile the post-sync population with marketing eligibility and the current billing rules before approving migration. A customer record, an active marketing subscriber and a billable profile are questions to resolve explicitly, not interchangeable counts.</p>
        <h2>When another lifecycle layer helps</h2>
        <p>If considering Sequenzy alongside either product, identify a specific missing task and demonstrate its data path first. Require evidence of how it would receive customer state and control pending actions. Adding a third tool does not itself coordinate offers or suppress duplicate recovery. A simpler configuration that your operator can explain and maintain may better serve this particular workflow.</p>
      </article>
      <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Drip FAQ" />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
